Of course you are right, and I apologize for the bad 
formulation above.  It's better to state that 'any 
strategy which requires luck in a way independent 
of the former actions and having a failure chance 
larger than a fixed number p > will eventually fail'.  

In our situation, we can model the amount of food in 
the ice-box as a Markov chain.  If there is an upper 
limit to the amount of corpses you may squeeze in 
a box, the strategy will fail eventually, if there 
is no such upper limit, I don't know for sure what 
the long-term probabilities are.  I would guess that 
it heavily depends on the ratio of 'turns to be waited 
until next monster is generated' against 'chance of 
getting a fire giant'.  But of course, it's always 
less than 1 ;-).  

